<article class="help-article p-4">
    <header class="help-article-header mb-4">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined help-article-icon">waving_hand</span>
        <h1 class="help-article-title">Welcome to Singularity</h1>
        <p class="help-article-lead">
            Singularity is the authoring environment for the Sabatier stack &mdash; an IDE for designing
            data models and generating complete PHP web service projects.
        </p>
    </header>

    <p>
        With Singularity you design your application's data model visually &mdash; its entities, their
        attributes, and the relationships between them &mdash; and Singularity generates a fully working
        PHP web service from that model. There is no boilerplate to write by hand.
    </p>

    <h2 class="help-heading">What you can do</h2>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li>Create and open projects, each backed by a folder on disk.</li>
        <li>Design a model made of <strong>entities</strong>, <strong>attributes</strong>, <strong>relationships</strong>, fetch requests, composite types, and configurations.</li>
        <li>Visualize your model as an interactive entity-relationship graph.</li>
        <li>Save model files, generate managed-object subclasses, inspect SQL output, and import existing models.</li>
        <li>Use the AI Copilot to ask questions or make model changes in the selected context.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="help-callout">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined">lightbulb</span>
        <div>
            <strong>Self-referential by design.</strong>
            Singularity is itself built with the Sabatier stack &mdash; it is a proof of concept of what
            you can build with the projects it generates.
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Where to next</h2>
    <p>
        If this is your first time here, start by
        <a class="help-link" href="#create-project" data-help-anchor="create-project">creating your first project</a>.
        Once you have a project open, learn how to
        <a class="help-link" href="#editor" data-help-anchor="editor">navigate the Editor</a>.
    </p>
</article>
